# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LISAT website — environment
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in the value below, then restart the
# dev server. `.env.local` is ignored by git (see .gitignore) and must never be
# committed; this example file has no real values in it and is safe to commit.
#
# Without these set, the site still builds and runs — it reads the team from
# the files in src/data, exactly as it did before Sanity existed.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Your Sanity project ID. Find it at https://www.sanity.io/manage — open the
# "LISAT WEBSITE" project and it is in the URL and on the project page.
#
# This is NOT a password. It is visible in every request the browser makes to
# Sanity and is public by design. Access is controlled by the project's member
# list, not by keeping this secret.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SANITY_PROJECT_ID=

# The dataset to read. Leave as `production` unless you have made another one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SANITY_DATASET=production

# Optional. Pins the Sanity API version so a future Sanity release cannot
# change the shape of a response. Defaults to 2024-11-01 if unset.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SANITY_API_VERSION=2024-11-01

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT NEEDED, AND DELIBERATELY ABSENT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# There is no API token here. The website only reads published documents from a
# public dataset, which needs no credentials at all. If one is ever required —
# for previewing unpublished drafts, say — it goes in a variable WITHOUT the
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, for example:
#
#   SANITY_API_READ_TOKEN=
#
# The prefix is what tells Next.js to inline a value into the JavaScript sent
# to the browser. A token with that prefix would be readable by anyone who
# opens the page source.
